The sequel to the hit XCOM: Enemy Unknown, it brings a number of things that are similar to the predecessor, but also different.  All in all, a solid XCOM game, albeit not as good as the predecessor in a number of ways.
In the XCOM predecessor, you head up an anti-alien organization put together to both stop and investigate alien attacks on earth.  In this sequel, the presumption is you lost that campaign, earth is under rule of an alien hierarchy, and your goal is to make as many insurgent attacks as possible while stopping the game's ultimate "doomsday" type device.
Positives:
1. More mission variety;
2. More things to do and keep track of;
3. Tougher game play
Negative:
1. More things to do and keep track of (see above);
2. Tougher gameplay (as in a mission timeer in about 75% of the missions)(see above);
The things that make this game different and at times are also what hold it back from being a great gaming experience.  While its good they changed the ultimate goal to make it more difficult (in the predecessor satellites was the way to go), in the end being rushed through things doesnt feel that fun - I have to deal with that enough in real life.  Message to the devs:  Mission timers are just one way to increase difficulty, but was way overused in this game.
Overall, its still a good XCOM experience and I recommend it for anyone who played the predecessor and liked it - just be prepared for an increase in difficulty.  If you have not and are wondering which one to go with first, go with the predecessor Enemy Unknown (and get the XPAC Enemy Within, its pretty cool).
BTW, TIP: be aware that the end game mission can be pretty brutal. You will face enemy group sizes you havent seen before. Make sure to bring at least one well trained psi op (I would actually recommend two) who has domination and void, etc.  If you get the right group with AOE damage (that refreshes, not limited in battle), the end mission is not so bad.
Finally, kudos on the music in this one.  I didnt think they could make a tune as good as "Ready for Battle!" from the predecessor, but they did in that very surreal sounding drum beat in "Ambush" (the last 90 seconds of the full music are awesome).

